{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"1b0f563909ec2a9108e033e2f915cc449641818416676a184451b315d50b3dd6","slug":"docetism","location":"/gospel/gnosticism/docetism.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Docetism","authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Eric Araujo","given":"Eric","family":"Araujo"},"name":"Eric Araujo","corresponding":true,"email":"eric.araujo@calvin.edu","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/Codex_Tchacos_p33-9ba693e09017dec05e18f65ee1982f4f.jpg","thumbnailOptimized":"/Codex_Tchacos_p33-9ba693e09017dec05e18f65ee1982f4f.webp","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"docetism.md","url":"/docetism-992285303c8986f58225ea8d3f639d33.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docetism was a prominent Gnostic belief that emerged in the early centuries of Christianity, asserting that Jesus Christ only ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"McVmRcs90j"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"appeared","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"rVvlQVeHN9"}],"key":"gqPJNAlR5W"},{"type":"text","value":" to have a physical body and did not truly possess one. The term “docetism” is derived from the Greek word “dokein,” which means “to seem” or “to appear.”","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"JU1GeMYY7a"}],"label":"docetism","identifier":"docetism","html_id":"docetism","key":"fwwEGE3Udj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"According to docetic teachings, Jesus was a purely spiritual being who merely took on the appearance of a human form, but his physical body was an illusion. This belief was rooted in the Gnostic worldview, which viewed the material world as inherently evil or corrupt, and therefore, it was inconceivable for a divine being to take on a physical body.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"VLbWMpj4Sl"}],"key":"OZuHn19nHY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docetism posed a significant challenge to orthodox Christian beliefs, particularly regarding the incarnation and the nature of Jesus Christ. The early church firmly rejected docetic teachings, emphasizing the full humanity and divinity of Jesus Christ as essential to the Christian faith. The ","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"Hfv85aydSp"},{"type":"crossReference","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Council of Nicaea","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"r5QitTAC68"}],"identifier":"nicaea","label":"nicaea","kind":"paragraph","template":"Paragraph","resolved":true,"html_id":"nicaea","remote":true,"url":"/nicaea","dataUrl":"/nicaea.json","key":"c3Onw1I7Zt"},{"type":"text","value":" in 325 CE and subsequent councils affirmed the orthodox understanding of Christ’s incarnation, rejecting docetism and other heresies that denied the true humanity of Jesus.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"sf8s9nznEP"}],"key":"r0GT2fTaN8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is important to note that docetism is best understood as a collection of widespread tendencies rather than a movement originating from one person.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"T8AyOyay9K"}],"key":"a3gQJNzghh"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/Codex_Tchacos_p33-9ba693e09017dec05e18f65ee1982f4f.jpg","alt":"The Gospel of Peter, a Gnostic text discovered in the Nag Hammadi library, contains docetic elements that reflect the belief that Jesus’ physical form was an illusion. Source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"zxk4BfpupG","urlSource":"../figures/Codex_Tchacos_p33.jpg","urlOptimized":"/Codex_Tchacos_p33-9ba693e09017dec05e18f65ee1982f4f.webp"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Gospel of Peter, a Gnostic text discovered in the Nag Hammadi library, contains docetic elements that reflect the belief that Jesus’ physical form was an illusion. ","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"LtDLhQfmzV"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Peter","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"rF8w2DoTIL"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Peter","data":{"page":"Gospel_of_Peter","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"dAZrSwc1pg"}],"key":"HG7ALLdOYq"}],"key":"XRUGNg80eO"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"BjsZy5uJ9X"}],"key":"fXfk4lPHGL"}],"key":"XcAMPvJAzq"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}